## Other adapter classification

The same classification gap was reproduced in PostgreSQL over PGlite, the
MySQL client seam backed by SQLite, and the D1 compensating journal over SQLite
(private issue 116). PostgreSQL and MySQL now distinguish work failure with a
confirmed rollback from an unconfirmed COMMIT. The journal records whether its
rollback actually executed; a missing ownership lock after an uncertain commit
does not prove that another holder restored the journal. A refused atomic commit
whose journal is still owned can be rolled back and remains a definite failure.
Corrected controls are retained in `.runtime/unknown-other-adapters-before-final.json`;
the first fixture attempts are retained separately and do not establish product
failures. No native MySQL or hosted D1 acceptance is implied by these controls.

## Recoverable derived assets and terminal stream cleanup

Independent re-attack found two additional paths (private issues 117 and 119).
The original 117 control lost the only preimage of an overwritten stylesheet
after an unknown rollback. The original 119 controls left an uncommitted Hrana
stream holding its database lock when COMMIT never reached the server. Raw
failed controls are `.runtime/unknown-asset-before.json` (one failure) and
`.runtime/unknown-commit-close-before.json` (two failures).

Before replacing an existing derived output, the engine now stores its prior
bytes under a unique recovery extension through the same attachment store.
Recovery copies use the normal files area, not `tmp/`, whose abandoned-upload
sweep deletes by age. The original and replacement SHA-256 values and the
target/backup identities are carried in `reconciliation.recoveries`.

An unknown outcome retains both the replacement and the recovery copy. A
confirmed rollback restores the old bytes before discarding its backup; a
confirmed commit discards only the backup. A confirmed commit whose backup
cleanup fails reports `save-committed-cleanup-pending`, `outcome: committed`,
and `retrySafe: false`, rather than claiming the save failed or rolled back.
The HTTP message distinguishes that state from an unknown database result.
Stores without discard/remove support expose that cleanup failure explicitly.

`reconcileAssetRecoveries` is the first-class file reconciliation operation.
Its caller must first confirm the database outcome by reading the reported
card/action history. It rejects an unknown outcome. For confirmed rollback it
verifies both the backup and the current replacement hashes, refuses to
overwrite a later save's different bytes, restores in reverse write order,
and retains all backups until restoration finishes. A confirmed commit keeps
the replacement and removes its recovery copies. No background service chooses
the outcome or replays a save.

Both Hrana adapters now attempt close-only cleanup using their last known baton
after a terminal request fails. COMMIT is never replayed. If COMMIT did not
arrive, close releases its still-open transaction; if it already committed and
closed, the failed close is retained with the original uncertainty. A cleanup
attempt does not turn an unknown commit into a claimed rollback.

The two unchanged new attack files pass with these repairs. Targeted asset,
reconciliation and stream checks recorded 17 passes, no skips, including a
refusal to overwrite a later save. The broader local Bunny/libSQL/atomic-save
selection recorded 98 passes and 62 credential/filter skips. Full TypeScript
typechecking passed. These scopes remain local; provider qualification is
unchanged.

## Conditional restoration custody

Independent issue 122 reproduced a write landing after the recovery read took
its snapshot. The former hash check authorized an unconditional store, which
overwrote the newer bytes and deleted the backup. The unchanged attacker control
is retained as `tests/card/actions/asset-recovery-concurrent-write.attack.test.ts`;
the raw failing record is `.runtime/asset-recovery-race-before.json`.

Rollback reconciliation now requires `AttachmentStore.restoreIfUnchanged`.
The storage implementation must bind comparison of the expected current bytes
to replacement using a genuine conditional write or exclusive custody honored
by every writer. A read followed by unconditional store is explicitly not that
capability. The helper checks the returned outcome and retains backups on
refusal or failure; even an already-restored read snapshot does not bypass this
requirement before cleanup.

The current BlobAttachmentStore exposes no conditional-write capability, so
automatic rollback reconciliation on it reports
`asset-recovery-custody-required` and preserves both target and backup. This
change does not claim a conditional-write implementation for a native or hosted
provider. Confirmed-commit backup cleanup remains available. The local positive
control uses a synthetic map whose comparison and replacement execute together
without an intervening await; a separate control lands a conflicting write at
that conditional operation and verifies refusal.

The original race control now passes unchanged. Additional checks cover absent
capability, conditional rejection, unknown-outcome refusal and retained backups.
These controls establish guarded behavior, not hosted storage qualification.
Read buffers are copied before hashing and conditional replacement, so a store
returning a borrowed byte buffer cannot change the expected comparison bytes
after verification. The final local race/reconciliation/style selection recorded
19 passes with no skips; full TypeScript typechecking passed.

## PostgreSQL and MySQL terminal cleanup

Independent issue 125 found that COMMIT classification alone left a PostgreSQL
transaction open when its request was never delivered. The before-control
reported uncommitted `n=1` on the same actual PGlite connection and accepted a
SAVEPOINT after the driver returned its error. The final before-regression
record is `.runtime/terminal-commit-cleanup-before.json`: four failing controls,
covering before-delivery and consumed-successful-reply paths in PostgreSQL/PGlite
and the SQLite-backed MySQL client seam.

Both drivers now attempt ROLLBACK after COMMIT rejection while still holding
their transaction gate. They preserve the original unknown outcome and never
resend COMMIT: cleanup after a commit already landed cannot undo that commit.
If rollback cleanup fails, they retire an owned client before returning and
retain the original, rollback and shutdown errors. A retired driver rejects
queries, batches and subsequent transactions instead of serving an uncertain
connection. PostgreSQL clients explicitly marked borrowed are not forcibly
closed; the driver becomes unusable and reports that its connection owner must
release the client. A failed shutdown is retained as an error, not a claim that
the connection was released.

The unchanged lead probe was rerun from this checkout against the repaired
source. It observed `BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK`, refused SAVEPOINT outside a
transaction, and read `n=0` before its own final probe cleanup. Its receipt is
`.runtime/postgres-undelivered-commit-reproduction.json`.
The paired consumed-reply controls retain committed `n=1`. A separate subsequent
transaction succeeds, and fallback controls verify actual owned PGlite closure
or the MySQL client-seam disconnect and refusal of further driver operations.
The local terminal/adapter/PostgreSQL selection recorded 29 passes, no skips;
full TypeScript typechecking passed. These controls establish local PostgreSQL
WASM and client-seam behavior, not native PostgreSQL/MySQL server qualification.
